WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.990
So, now let's talk about the claw.md,

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.150
which going back to our analogy is sort

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.190
of like the employee handbook that your

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.310
agent reads every time you start a

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.110
session. And if you already have set up

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.910
your workspace, it is very likely that

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.630
you already have some sort of claw.md in

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.710
your files and workspace. So, you can

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.870
see for me I have this claw.md and

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.750
whenever you open a new session, this

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.630
claw.md, this markdown text file, is

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.550
going to be read by Claude Code, which

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.310
now becomes instructions for the agent

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.270
every time that you start a chat session

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.310
with it. In my view, there are basically

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.990
four things that a good claw.md should

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.830
do well. The very first thing is

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.950
identity. So, it should establish the

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.110
identity of not just the agent that

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.190
you're talking to, but also some context

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.150
on who you are. Sort of tied to this is

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.990
the second purpose where you need to

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.110
provide Claude the specific goals that

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.350
you have, which you want it to remember

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.270
every time. And just to give you a view

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.830
or an example of my own claw.md, you can

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.910
see it doesn't need to be that complex.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.390
So, the identity portion here is

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.190
literally just three lines. You don't

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.910
have to give your agent a name, but I

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.910
think that is a bit more fun to do it.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.030
So, I named my Robo has a role around

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.590
being a chief of staff and I also

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.550
included a line around the vibe of the

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.150
agent, which matters more than you

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.790
think, especially if you're building out

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.630
this agent for a client or another

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.190
person. And the second component around

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.630
the goals also doesn't have to be super

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.190
detailed as you would expect. So, you

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.470
can see here just to illustrate some

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.590
trimmed down version of my goals, which

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:24.870
you can just put in here because what's

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:26.030
important when you're talking with

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.230
Claude Code is that it has knowledge at

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:29.750
least of what it is that you are trying

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.230
to achieve. So, that let's say if you're

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.350
asking about a specific portion of your

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.350
work, it knows exactly what is the end

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.590
in mind for you so that it can advise

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:38.910
against that. So, you can see here that

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.190
I have a conversation filter that for

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.430
every suggestion that it runs through,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.030
does this actually move the needle

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.270
towards those goals? And you can be as

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.710
detailed as you want, obviously, but

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.350
just having these two portions, I find

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.470
largely improves the way that you work

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.590
with your agents. Now, those two parts

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.150
are pretty simple, but the third

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.190
component, the rules part, is really the

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.910
version of the employee handbook that

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.390
you provide to your agent because that's

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.350
the part that really tells your agent

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.390
how to act, how to behave, what not to

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.550
touch, and essentially how you want it

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:09.950
to work. And so, at least for this

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.710
Claude.md that I have, you can see here

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.430
there are a couple of rules here around

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.390
writing, just making sure that it avoids

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.110
m dashes, which is a pretty common tell

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:18.630
when it comes to AI writing, always

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.670
writing in plain English, non-technical,

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.430
because that is also the way that I

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.190
personally want to teach, especially for

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.949
those who are not developers or

00:02:25.959 --> 00:02:27.750
non-coders. There's rules around

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.510
approvals, especially since if you're

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.949
using generative AI models, which cost

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:33.510
some credits whenever you invoke them,

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.030
there's rules here to always seek for

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.230
approval before executing that type of

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:38.870
commands. And it is obviously different

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.430
depending on how you work. And honestly,

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:41.949
the way that you use Claude Code will

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:43.910
mostly dictate what rules will be

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.550
included here. And a good practice to

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.510
adopt is whenever you work with Claude

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.070
Code and you think that a rule should be

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.270
in place, then just ask Claude to edit

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.550
its own Claude.md and add it in. Now,

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.670
I'll be providing this whole Claude.md

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.230
for you, including a prompt that will

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.150
basically just let your own Claude Code

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.110
self-assess what it can improve

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.990
according to these four pillars. But I

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.430
think one thing that is helpful,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.670
regardless of what workspace you use, is

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.550
this rule where you just ask Claude to

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.070
give suggestions to its own Claude.md

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:12.710
whenever it makes sense. Now, the fourth

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.910
pillar of what makes a good Claude.md is

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:17.350
that it also functions as a map of your

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.710
own workspace or second brain. And just

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.350
to show you mine, you can see here that

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.670
under the map section, it gave itself a

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.949
list here of the different domains of my

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:27.710
personal business with content,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.990
community, product development, design,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.990
operations, and it also gave itself a

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.509
map of where exactly in our workspace

00:03:34.519 --> 00:03:36.710
folder it can find projects in that

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.670
regard. So, obviously, this whole thing,

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.830
it is customized to my own setup, but

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.229
the key takeaway that you should have

00:03:42.239 --> 00:03:44.470
here is that you need to have some sort

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.270
of structured map for your own

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.710
workspace, especially as your context

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.350
and your memory starts to build up with

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.350
the intention that Claude Code will be

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.750
able to freely navigate and find the

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.070
things that you want instead of it being

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.390
lost in your own workspace. Again, this

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.150
is something that you don't really need

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.430
to overthink because the more that you

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.590
use Claude, it's only really necessary

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:08.110
to self-update your Claude.md. If you're

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.590
finding that it is not working up to

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:12.990
spec or is doing things that is not

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.790
aligned to what it is that you want. But

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.349
if your Claude.md, your employee

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:18.870
handbook, has at least a view of your

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.069
identity, the goals that you set for it,

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:22.830
and the rules and the map for your

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.390
workspace, then you'll be able to go far

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:29.080
with just a simple Claude.md.
